import DubC2.EntropyBound

/-!
# Dimension bounds for three uncertified cells at `3/2`, `ν = 0` (T6(b) instances)

None of `{2,5}`, `{2,5,7}`, `{2,7}` is certified at `(3/2, ν = 0)` — real avoiders may exist —
but each has `λ_det < 3/2`, and a kernel-checked `EntCert` (data generated by
`DubC2/entemit.py`) turns the ledger of `DubC2/README.md` into a theorem about real numbers:

| `𝒫` | `D` | states | `λ_det` | `a/b` | `dim_H ≤` |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `{2,5}` | 2 | 10 | 1.324718 (plastic number) | 53/40 | 0.6940 < 7/10 |
| `{2,5,7}` | 2 | 32 | 1.210608 | 109/90 | 0.4724 < 12/25 |
| `{2,7}` | 8 | 40 | 1.285199 | 9/7 | 0.6198 < 5/8 |

Each statement: the real `ξ` such that `⌊ξ(3/2)ⁿ⌋` is eventually prime to every member of `𝒫`
form a set of Hausdorff dimension at most the stated bound.  (Lebesgue-null is classical, from
the a.e. equidistribution of `ξ(3/2)ⁿ/ℓ` mod 1; the dimension bound is not.)  At `𝒫 = {2}` the
model has `λ_det = 3/2` and the bound is the vacuous `1`: Mahler's problem is untouched.
-/

namespace DubC2.Atlas

